=ECE605 Large-Scale AI

== Course Overview
The Fall 2026 offering focuses on the Embodied AI and Visual-Language-Action models.
The course emphasizes real-world research problem-solving through three core pillars:
- Literature Review: Role-playing presentations dissecting frontier across five thematic clusters:
    - Final Project Foundations & Interactive Simulation
    - Vision-Language-Action (VLA) Architectures
    - Robotics Data Systems & Scaling Laws
    - World Models & Latent Planning
    - Hybrid & Multimodal FrontiersVision-Language-Action (VLA) architectures and generative control policies.
- Rigorous Experimentation: Hands-on empirical exploration, baseline reproduction, and model training on GPU clusters.
- Collaborative Teamwork: Large-team engineering to build, iterate, and submit competitive solutions to a live benchmark.

== Class schedule
- Monday and Wednesday, 10:30-11:45, Moore Hall 203 ([https://hawaii.zoom.us/j/84384222329 zoom link]; password: ece)
- Office hours: by [https://calendar.app.google/mLp9CvS96utpEjDK6 appointment] or after class

== Prerequisites
- *Essential background*: [https://igormolybog.github.io/ece405_sprg2026.html ECE405 Introduction to Large-Scale AI Systems] (undergraduate version). Mastery of the material covered in ECE405 (or equivalent) is crucial for success in ECE605. If you have not taken it, please review the ECE405 lecture slides, recordings, and assignments.

